# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env.
# Grouped as in the README's Configuration section: the bridge, then each webhook provider. The last two
# groups are only for working on this repo (the e2e tests and the reference deployment).

# === The bridge (every deployment) ===
# Project API key, for a dedicated, non-production Hookdeck project.
HOOKDECK_API_KEY=
# Project signing secret: verifies Event Gateway's signature on requests to the bridge.
# Dashboard: Project settings > Secrets.
HOOKDECK_SIGNING_SECRET=
# Secret path segment of the MCP URL (https://<bridge>/mcp/<secret>); the URL is a credential.
# Leave empty and `npm run bridge -- setup` generates one and prints the URL to paste into ChatGPT.
BRIDGE_MCP_SECRET=
# How provider events reach the bridge: `cli` locally (hookdeck listen, no public URL) or `http` when
# deployed (the default on Fly.io).
BRIDGE_INBOUND=cli
# For http inbound only; on Fly.io the default is https://$FLY_APP_NAME.fly.dev.
# BRIDGE_PUBLIC_URL=
# One HTTP listener for inbound routes and the MCP endpoint.
BRIDGE_PORT=8080

# === Webhook providers (only the ones this repo's bridge.config.ts uses) ===

# --- Resend ---
# API key that can create webhooks. setup registers the webhook with it.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# --- GitHub (optional) ---
# This repo's bridge.config.ts enables GitHub when one of these is set (also set it on a deployed bridge):
# - GITHUB_REPOS (comma-separated owner/name): setup registers the webhooks with GITHUB_TOKEN, a fine-grained
#   token with the Webhooks (read and write) permission on those repositories. Only setup uses the token.
# - GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET (manual mode, at least 16 characters): add webhooks to any repositories yourself, with
#   the source URL `npm run bridge -- setup` prints and this secret. Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
# GITHUB_REPOS=
# GITHUB_TOKEN=
# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Generic webhooks (optional) ---
# Each webhook() instance's credentials, named by its env() references; `mcp-events-bridge providers add webhook <id>`
# appends <ID>_WEBHOOK_SECRET (or _API_KEY, or _USERNAME and _PASSWORD) here for you. Also set them on a deployed bridge.
# This repo's bridge.config.ts enables the README's `fills` example (HMAC-SHA256, hex, x-signature) when
# FILLS_WEBHOOK_SECRET is set; E2E_WEBHOOK=1 npm run e2e signs test fills with it. Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
# FILLS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# === Development and tests (this repo only) ===
# npm run e2e sends real email through Resend; E2E_SOURCE=webhook sends signed fills instead (needs FILLS_WEBHOOK_SECRET),
# so it sends no email. Flags such as E2E_GITHUB=1, E2E_WEBHOOK=1 and E2E_EXTENDED=1 are described at the top of scripts/e2e.ts.
# Any address on your Resend account's receiving domain, e.g. test@<id>.resend.app
# (Resend dashboard: Emails > Receiving > ... > Receiving address).
RESEND_INBOUND_ADDRESS=
# A sender address on your verified Resend sending domain (RESEND_API_KEY must be able to send email).
RESEND_TEST_FROM=

# --- Hermes Agent (optional) ---
# For running Hermes Agent from its MCP Events pull request against this bridge, by hand
# (skills/mcp-events-bridge/references/hermes-agent.md). Hermes needs a model to act on an event: put this key in
# Hermes's own .env as ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (Hermes provider "anthropic"). Use a key with a spending limit.
HERMES_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# The model Hermes uses; a small one is enough to react to a test email.
# HERMES_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001

# === Reference deployment (maintainers) ===
# Fly.io API token for deploying the reference app in fly.toml.
FLY_API_TOKEN=
